L3: Procedures and best practices for trigonometric leveling in the U.S. Geological Survey
L5: December 23, 2020
L10: With the advent of highly precise total stations and modern surveying instrumentation, trigonometric leveling has become a compelling alternative to conventional leveling methods for establishing vertical-control networks and for perpetuating a datum to field sites. Previous studies of trigonometric-leveling measurement uncertainty proclaim that first-, second-, and third-order accuracies may be achieved if strict leveling protocols are rigorously observed.
L11: Common field techniques include averaging zenith angles and slope distances observed in direct and reverse instrument orientation (F1/F2), multiple reciprocal observations, meteorological observations to correct atmospheric refraction, and electronic distance measurements generally not exceeding 500 feet.
L12: Specifications concern differences between F1/F2 zenith angles and slope distances, redundant instrument heights, reciprocal observations and traverse closure.
L13: Recommended trigonometric-leveling equipment includes a total station with angular uncertainty at most ±5 arc-seconds and integrated electronic distance measurement uncertainty at most ±3 mm plus 3 ppm.
L14: Paired collector/microprocessor averages multiple measurement sets in direct/reverse orientation; redundant measurements and slant-height reduction are recommended.
L15-L24: Daily collimation checks, traverse types, sideshots, quality controls, field equipment and survey method dependencies are described in the full page response.
L25-L35: Publication metadata: year 2020, title, DOI 10.3133/tm11D3, authors Michael L. Noll and Paul H. Rydlund, USGS Numbered Series Techniques and Methods 11-D3.
L38-L68: Full response includes contact information and USGS navigation.

